 ## Overview
-Adapted Splatting is a volume rendering method that optimizes standard splatting by culling voxels invisible to the observer and using a compressed data format to reduce memory usage for free voxels [S1, unofficial]. It solves the high computational cost of processing all voxels in a model and the large memory footprint of storing sparse volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard splatting by introducing a two-pass optimization: a depth-buffer-based occlusion culling pass that identifies only visible voxels, followed by rendering only those selected voxels, while simultaneously employing a run-length-like encoding for storage that groups consecutive free voxels [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Splatting and Point-based Rendering](4975-splatting-and-point-based-rendering.md) and differs from siblings such as [Ray-Box Intersection via Quad Rasterization](2253-ray-box-intersection-via-quad-rasterization.md), [Sparse Voxel Octree Rendering via Square Splats](2993-sparse-voxel-octree-rendering-via-square-splats.md), [Voxel Point Sprite Rendering](4307-voxel-point-sprite-rendering.md), and [Differentiable Point-Based Radiance Fields](7366-differentiable-point-based-radiance-fields.md) by relying on explicit voxel culling and a custom segment-based storage layout rather than hierarchical traversal or differentiable field representations.
